---
<Layout title_cy="Hanes Llansannan" title_en="History of Llansannan">
  <div class="page-banner"><h1><span lang="cy">Hanes Llansannan</span><span lang="en">History of Llansannan</span></h1></div>
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/"><span lang="cy">Hafan</span><span lang="en">Home</span></a> &rsaquo;
    <a href="/llansannan/">Llansannan</a> &rsaquo;
    <span lang="cy">Hanes</span><span lang="en">History</span>
  </div>
  <div class="site-wrap"><div class="content" data-pagefind-body>
    <h2 class="sh"><span lang="cy">Hanes yr Ardal</span><span lang="en">Local History</span></h2><hr class="sh-rule">

    <h3 class="sub-sh"><span lang="cy">Yr Eglwys a'r Plwyf</span><span lang="en">The Church and Parish</span></h3>
    <p lang="cy">Cafodd pentref Llansannan ei enwi ar ôl Sant Sannan, ac mae Eglwys Sant Sannan yn ganolbwynt i fywyd y gymuned ers canrifoedd. Mae'r plwyf yn ymestyn ar draws tirwedd brydferth Dyffryn Aled.</p>
    <p lang="en">The village of Llansannan takes its name from Saint Sannan, and St Sannan's Church has been central to community life for centuries. The parish extends across the beautiful landscape of the Aled Valley.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-sh"><span lang="cy">William Salesbury</span><span lang="en">William Salesbury</span></h3>
    <p lang="cy">Mae Llansannan yn enwog fel man geni William Salesbury (c.1520–c.1584), a gyfieithodd y Testament Newydd i'r Gymraeg yn 1567 — cyfraniad anferth at gadw'r Gymraeg yn fyw. Mae cofeb iddo yn y pentref.</p>
    <p lang="en">Llansannan is celebrated as the birthplace of William Salesbury (c.1520–c.1584), who translated the New Testament into Welsh in 1567 — a monumental contribution to the survival of the Welsh language. A memorial to him stands in the village.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-sh"><span lang="cy">Twm o'r Nant</span><span lang="en">Twm o'r Nant</span></h3>
    <p lang="cy">Roedd Thomas Edwards (1739–1810), a adwaenid fel "Twm o'r Nant", yn fardd ac anterliwtiwr enwog o'r ardal. Fe'i disgrifiwyd fel "Molière Cymru" oherwydd ei ddramâu poblogaidd.</p>
    <p lang="en">Thomas Edwards (1739–1810), known as "Twm o'r Nant", was a celebrated poet and playwright from the area. He was described as the "Molière of Wales" for his popular interludes.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-sh"><span lang="cy">Bro Aled</span><span lang="en">Bro Aled</span></h3>
    <p lang="cy">Mae ardal Bro Aled, sy'n cwmpasu Llansannan a'r cymunedau cyfagos, yn ardal o draddodiad Cymraeg cryf. Denwyd y Gymraeg yma o genhedlaeth i genhedlaeth drwy farddonaeth, capeli a phatrwm amaethyddol yr ardal.</p>
    <p lang="en">The Bro Aled area, encompassing Llansannan and surrounding communities, is a stronghold of Welsh tradition. The Welsh language has been transmitted here from generation to generation through poetry, chapels and the agricultural pattern of the area.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:2rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--text-muted)" lang="cy">Gwybodaeth hanesyddol i'w ychwanegu gan y clerc — cysylltwch â ni i gyfrannu.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:2rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--text-muted)" lang="en">Historical content to be expanded by the clerk — contact us to contribute.</p>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
